{"candidates": [{"conjecture": "The optimal configuration is 4 points at the corners of the unit square, 4 points at the midpoints of the sides, and 5 points placed to maximize the minimum area, achieving a minimum triangle area of approximately 0.0089. The corner-midpoint structure forces all small triangles to involve at least one interior point, and the interior points are positioned to equalize the smallest areas.", "probability": 0.085}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration achieves a minimum triangle area of exactly 1/112 ≈ 0.008929, attained by a configuration with points on a perturbed triangular lattice structure adapted to the square boundary. The value 1/112 arises from the combinatorial constraint that 286 triangles must each have area at least 1/112, which is tight for a near-uniform distribution.", "probability": 0.078}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration places all 13 points on the boundary of the unit square—specifically 4 at corners and 9 distributed along edges—and achieves a minimum triangle area of 1/108 ≈ 0.009259. Boundary-only configurations avoid the small triangles that interior points create with near-collinear neighbors.", "probability": 0.072}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration is a 13-point triangular lattice (hexagonal packing) clipped to the unit square, with boundary adjustments, achieving minimum triangle area of approximately 0.0085. The hexagonal structure minimizes the ratio of small-area triangles, and boundary perturbation recovers area lost to the square's geometry.", "probability": 0.069}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration has 4 points at corners, 4 at edge midpoints, and 5 at the vertices of a centered regular pentagon scaled to equalize all minimum-area triangles, achieving a minimum area of exactly 1/120 ≈ 0.008333. The pentagonal symmetry of the interior points balances the rectangular symmetry of the boundary.", "probability": 0.064}, {"conjecture": "The optimal minimum triangle area for 13 points in the unit square is exactly 1/126 ≈ 0.007937, achieved by a configuration with nontrivial symmetry group D2 (180-degree rotational symmetry). One point sits at the center, 4 at corners, and 8 arranged in symmetric pairs, with the minimum-area triangles all being of one structural type.", "probability": 0.061}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration is the 13-point Fibonacci spiral (sunflower arrangement) scaled to the unit square, achieving a minimum triangle area of approximately 0.0091. The logarithmic spiral distribution provably minimizes the smallest triangle by equalizing angular gaps, outperforming lattice-based approaches near boundaries.", "probability": 0.055}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration is a 4×3 grid plus one centered point, with grid spacing 1/3 and the extra point at (1/2, 1/2), but with all points perturbed by a specific optimization vector, achieving minimum triangle area of 1/99 ≈ 0.010101. The grid provides the backbone and the perturbation breaks the collinearities that would otherwise create zero-area triangles.", "probability": 0.052}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration achieves minimum triangle area of approximately 0.0096 and has no nontrivial symmetry—the optimal arrangement is generic. Symmetric configurations are suboptimal because the square boundary and triangular lattice are incompatible, so the optimizer breaks all symmetry to distribute area-deficient triangles more evenly.", "probability": 0.048}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration places 13 points at the vertices of a maximal-area regular 13-gon inscribed in the largest circle fitting the unit square (radius 1/2), then perturbs outward toward corners, achieving minimum triangle area of approximately 0.0075. The regular polygon provides uniform triangle areas internally, and corner-pulling compensates for boundary inefficiency.", "probability": 0.044}]}
